# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
aristotle-ethics-4.md:1: argue/readings: S3 is read 2 ways: aristotle-ethics-4.logic.r2 has "diff_class_harder → O ¬give_up"; aristotle-ethics-4.logic.r2.2 and aristotle-ethics-4.logic.r2.3 have "diff_class_harder → ¬O give_up"; they differ when only "discriminating between those in different classes is a matter of more trouble than between those of the same class" is true, which the second allows
aristotle-ethics-4.md:1: argue/answered: S3: answered no, which keeps aristotle-ethics-4.logic.r2 and aristotle-ethics-4.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "discriminating between those in different classes is a matter of more trouble than between those of the same class" and "it ought to be that we give up the attempt to discriminate what belongs to each" are true and "it ought to be that we give up the attempt to discriminate what belongs to each" and "it ought to be that it is not so that we give up the attempt to discriminate what belongs to each" are false. Is S3, "This, however, should not be a reason for giving up the attempt, but we must observe the distinctions so far as it is practicable to do so.", true then?
aristotle-ethics-4.md:1: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 2 ways: aristotle-ethics-4.logic.r2 has "O(discriminate ∧ ¬give_up)"; aristotle-ethics-4.logic.r2.2 and aristotle-ethics-4.logic.r2.3 have "O discriminate ∧ ¬O give_up"; they differ when only "it ought to be that we discriminate, and observe the distinctions as to, what belongs to each in respect of nearness of connection, goodness or intimacy" is true, which the second allows
aristotle-ethics-4.md:1: argue/answered: the conclusion: answered no, which keeps aristotle-ethics-4.logic.r2 and aristotle-ethics-4.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "it ought to be that we give up the attempt to discriminate what belongs to each", "it ought to be that we discriminate, and observe the distinctions as to, what belongs to each in respect of nearness of connection, goodness or intimacy" and "it ought to be that we discriminate, and observe the distinctions as to, what belongs to each in respect of nearness of connection, goodness or intimacy" are true and "it ought to be that we give up the attempt to discriminate what belongs to each" and "it ought to be that (we discriminate, and observe the distinctions as to, what belongs to each in respect of nearness of connection, goodness or intimacy and it is not so that we give up the attempt to discriminate what belongs to each)" are false. Is the conclusion, "This, however, should not be a reason for giving up the attempt, but we must observe the distinctions so far as it is practicable to do so.", true then?
aristotle-ethics-4.md:1: argue/unfaithful: aristotle-ethics-4.logic.r2 P4 (S3): the sentence has no word of "discriminating between those in different classes is a matter of more trouble than between those of the same class": is it the sentence P4 came from?
aristotle-ethics-4.md:1: argue/unfaithful: aristotle-ethics-4.logic.r2.2 P6 (S3): the sentence has no word of "discriminating between those in different classes is a matter of more trouble than between those of the same class": is it the sentence P6 came from?
